.TH std::layout_stride::mapping::mapping-traits 3 "2026.11.10" "http://cppreference.com" "C++ Standard Libary"
.SH NAME
std::layout_stride::mapping::mapping-traits \- std::layout_stride::mapping::mapping-traits

.SH Synopsis
static constexpr bool is_unique() noexcept;            \fB(1)\fP (since C++23)
constexpr bool is_exhaustive() const noexcept;         \fB(2)\fP (since C++23)
static constexpr bool is_strided() noexcept;           \fB(3)\fP (since C++23)
static constexpr bool is_always_unique() noexcept;     \fB(4)\fP (since C++23)
static constexpr bool is_always_exhaustive() noexcept; \fB(5)\fP (since C++23)
static constexpr bool is_always_strided() noexcept;    \fB(6)\fP (since C++23)

Every instance of every specialization of mapping is unique and strided.

The mapping is exhaustive if one of the following conditions is true:

  * rank_ is 0, or
  * there exists a permutation p of the integers in the range [0, rank_) such that:

           * stride(p[0]) equals 1 and
           * stride(p[i]) equals stride(p[i - 1]) * extents().extent(p[i - 1])
        for all i in [1, rank_), where p[i] is the i^th element of p.

(rank_ is an exposition-only static member constant defined in
std::layout_stride::mapping.)

See LayoutMapping for the semantics of these predicate mapping traits.

.SH Parameters

\fI(none)\fP

.SH Return value

1,3-4,6) true
2) true if the mapping is exhaustive (see above)
5) false

